[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s firewall (iptables) on the inter-site uplink. The forwarding policy on router6 is dropping all traffic that arrives from its WAN/uplink, preventing return packets and any inbound flows from the rest of the network.

Mechanism: PCs in 172.18.51.0/24 reach their default gateway (router6 eth1 = 172.18.51.254), but any traffic to “outside” relies on replies coming back via router6 eth2 (10.50.1.2). Router6’s FORWARD chain has blanket DROP rules for any packets with incoming interface eth2, so return traffic is discarded. This makes it appear that “everything outside has vanished” from that office.

Localization: Router6 (interfaces: eth1 = 172.18.51.254 LAN, eth2 = 10.50.1.2 uplink to router3). Evidence:
- pc15 can ping 172.18.51.254 (LAN GW), but cross-site pings time out.
- Router0 can ping router6 at 172.18.51.254, but cannot reach a host behind it (172.18.51.15) since forwarding is blocked.
- Router6 shows ip_forward=1 and correct routes, but iptables -S reveals:
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
Only a very narrow exception exists for TCP to 172.18.51.1:80, effectively shutting out all other traffic.

Root cause: Misconfigured iptables on router6 that drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2.

Corrective fix: Remove or adjust the eth2 DROP rules on router6. Recommended: flush the FORWARD chain or at minimum add “-m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT” and explicit ACCEPTs for eth1<->eth2, then remove the catch-all eth2 DROP entries. Verify inter-site reachability after changes. === END ===